(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-02-frequency-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 2: Frequency Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A study was conducted of bladder cancer cases in men. Cancer registries report 40 new cases of bladder cancer per 100,000 men per year. Cases were from a complete count of all patients who developed bladder cancer in several regions of the United States, and the number of men at risk was estimated from the census data in those regions. Which rate is this an example of?","choices":["Point prevalence","Incidence density","Period prevalence","Cumulative incidence","Complication rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The population at risk is dynamic—people are entering and leaving it continually, so the rates from cancer registries should be reported as person- years. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.1"},{"q":"You are reading a study of the prevalence of uterine cervix infections and want to decide if the study is scientifically sound. Which of the following is not important?","choices":["Participants are followed up for a sufficient period of time for infection to occur.","The study is done on a representative sample of the population.","All members of the population are women.","Cervical infection is clearly defined.","The study is done on a sample from a defined population."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. There is no follow-up (no time dimension) in a prevalence study. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.2"},{"q":"A probability sample of a defined population:","choices":["Is invalidated by oversampling.","Is inferior to a random sample.","Is not representative of the population.","Results in a representative sample of the population only if there are enough people in the sample."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A random or probability sample is representative of the population sampled in the long run, if enough people in the population are sampled. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.3"},{"q":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) would like to know the average duration of her disease. You cannot find a study that directly answers that question. However, other studies show a prevalence of 1.5%, and annual incidence of 50/100,000. These rates have been stable. On average, how many years does the disease persist?","choices":["15","25","30","33","50"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a steady state, duration of disease = prevalence/incidence. In this case, it is 1.5/100 divided by 50/100,000/year = 30 years. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.4"},{"q":"Which of the following studies is not a cohort study?","choices":["The proportion of patients with stomach cancer who survive 5 years","The risk of developing diabetes mellitus in children according to their weight","Complications of influenza vaccine among children vaccinated in 2011","The earlier course of disease in a group of patients now under care in a clinic","Patients admitted to an intensive care unit and followed up for whether they are still alive at the time of hospital discharge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A study beginning with existing patients with a disease and looking back at their previous experience with the disease would not be a cohort study, which begins with patients with something in common (e.g., new onset of disease) and follows them forward in time for subsequent health events. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.5"},{"q":"A sample for a study of incidence of medication errors is obtained by enrolling every 10th patient admitted to a hospital. What kind of sample is this?","choices":["Stratified sample","Probability sample","Convenience sample","Random sample","Oversample"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Even though sampling every 10th patient might produce a representative sample, it would not be random and could misrepresent the population sampled. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.6"},{"q":"A study was performed to determine rates of hospital-acquired infection. Participants were 1,000 patients randomly selected from 150 hospitals in 5 countries. Over a 4-month period, surveys were conducted on a single day at each hospital (the date varied by hospital), and medical records were reviewed for that date to determine the presence of hospital-acquired infection. At least one hospital-acquired infection was found in 4% of patients. Which rate is this an example of?","choices":["Point prevalence","Complication rate","Incidence density","Cumulative incidence","Period prevalence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is point prevalence because cases are described for a point in time. The study assesses a single point in time for each person in the study—the actual measurement need not be on the same calendar day for all people in the study. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.7"},{"q":"Cohort studies of children with a first febrile seizure have shown that they have a one in three chance of having another seizure during childhood. What kind of rate is this?","choices":["Point prevalence","Cumulative incidence","Complication rate","Period prevalence","Incidence density"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Children with another seizure were all part of the original cohort of children with a first seizure. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.8"},{"q":"Which of the following would not increase the observed incidence of disease?","choices":["More aggressive efforts to detect the disease","A true increase in incidence","A more sensitive way of detecting the disease","A lowering of the threshold for diagnosis of disease","Studying a larger sample of the population"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A larger sample would produce an estimate of incidence that is closer to the true one by reducing random error. It would not introduce a systematic difference in incidence as A–D would. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.9"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man from Arizona presents to the emergency room with cough, fever, and shortness of breath for the past week. A chest radiograph is consistent with pneumonia. Among the considerations for the source of pneumonia is coccidioidomycosis, a fungus that is common in the deserts of the southwestern United States and in Mexico, but uncommon elsewhere. Which of the following best describes this infection?","choices":["Endemic","Pandemic","Incident","Epidemic","Prevalent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is endemic because it is confined to a geographic area. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.10"},{"q":"Twenty-six percent of adults report having experienced back pain lasting at least a day in the prior 3 months. Which of the following best describes this rate?","choices":["Cumulative incidence","Incidence density","Point prevalence","Complication rate","Period prevalence"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The cases existed any time in a 3-month period. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.11"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes a “dynamic population”?","choices":["It is rapidly increasing in size.","It is uniquely suited for cohort studies.","People are continually entering and leaving the population.","It is the basis for measuring cumulative incidence.","It is the best kind of population for a random sample."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dynamic populations, such as residents of the State of North Carolina, are continually turning over because of births and deaths as well as out- migration and in-migration. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.12"},{"q":"During a clinic visit, a mother wonders if her newborn could develop scoliosis (a deformity of the spine that becomes apparent after birth, most often in adolescence). To provide this estimate you review the literature. Which cohort would be most appropriate to answer her question?","choices":["Children who were referred to an orthopedic surgeon for treatment","Children born in North Carolina in 2012 and examined for scoliosis until they are adults","Children who were found to have scoliosis in a survey of children in North Carolina","Children who have scoliosis and are available for study","Children who were randomly sampled from the population of North Carolina in the spring of 2012"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Children in the cohort have in common being born in North Carolina in 2012 and are followed up for scoliosis as it develops over time. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.13"},{"q":"Which of the following are prevalence studies especially useful for?","choices":["Describing the incidence of disease","Studying diseases that resolve rapidly","Estimating the duration of disease","Describing the proportion of people in a defined population with the condition of interest","Establishing cause and effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevalence studies are not useful for diseases of short duration because there will be few cases at a point in time. They do not measure incidence and cannot provide much evidence bearing on cause. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.14"},{"q":"Last year, 800,000 Americans died of heart disease or stoke. Which of the following best describes this statistic?","choices":["Incidence density","Point prevalence","Cumulative incidence","Period prevalence","None of the above"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A number like 800,000 without an associated denominator is not a rate and is, therefore, not any of the incidence and prevalence rates given in A–D. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.15"}]};
